PLATFORM NOTES
Monday, March 9, 2026

Bundling is back

The 2010s were the unbundling decade. Every feature of the enterprise suite became a startup, every startup raised on the premise that best-of-breed beats good-enough, and the average mid-size company ended up with ninety software subscriptions and a procurement team whose job was to count them.

The pendulum is swinging. The proximate cause is cost: CFOs who spent 2023 and 2024 cutting seats have discovered that the cheapest seat is the one in a suite they already pay for. The deeper cause is that the AI layer rewards bundling. An assistant that can see the calendar, the documents, the tickets and the chat is more useful than four assistants that can each see one, and the suites have the data in one place.

Startups selling a point solution should read this carefully but not fearfully. The suites bundle well and integrate badly; their "good enough" modules are good enough for the median customer and useless for the one with a real problem. The point solution's defense is depth in a workflow the suite treats as a checkbox. The point solution's risk is being a checkbox itself.

Two tactical notes. First, if a suite vendor announces a "lite" version of what you do, treat it as validation and as a deadline: you have perhaps two years before the lite version is good enough for your smaller customers. Second, the counter-move to bundling is not to build a suite yourself; it is to make your product the one that the suite's assistant calls when the checkbox does not work.

